{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-24069",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941628/assets/9d9aa1e6ffac387c53c7a1b35217a51cd17db7242ddedec4ff72c229a73d5291.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$",
